# chan_memchan_spawn.tkr — the §10 end-to-end channel-across-threads fixture. It proves the landed
# spawn runtime + the C0b in-process channel transport communicate at the TEKO surface: a spawned
# producer thread sends 0..99 (i64) into an F2-resident memchan, the main thread pops them until the
# Closed edge, and the round-trip sum (4950) is verified. Values cross the boundary as fixed-width
# bytes via `uptr::__unwrap<i64>` (the §5 Part A address primitive); the channel handle crosses the
# spawn boundary by copy. NOTE: this exercises the transport + spawn directly (the generic
# chan<T>::make<K> / svc<Rx/Tx> DI surface is C4, honest-stopped on the value-marshalling
# prerequisite). Runs on the C backend.

Feature: chan_memchan_spawn — a spawned worker sends, main pops, values round-trip through a memchan

  Scenario: producer_consumer_sum — 100 i64 sent by a worker thread, popped by main, sum 4950
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
